 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Reflected Light Component Lookup](3521-reflected-light-component-lookup.md)
-Pre-calculates surface responses to directional lighting for combinations of material types and quantized gradient directions, storing results in a lookup table. It decouples expensive lighting calculations from the per-voxel rendering loop, allowing for realistic surface appearance in volume rendering. Choose this when rendering multiple-material volumes with specular highlights and directional lighting.
+Pre-calculates surface responses to directional lights for quantized gradient directions and material types, storing results in a lookup table. It supports directional lighting but requires table recalculation when lighting factors change.
+
+### [Gradient Quantization](3522-gradient-quantization.md)
+Quantizes the gradient direction space into a finite number of unit gradients to facilitate the lookup table indexing.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel for the classification step [G]. The overall rendering time depends on the volume rendering algorithm (e.g., ray casting) and the cost of the shading lookup (see [Reflected Light Component Lookup](3521-reflected-light-component-lookup.md)) [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel for the classification logic [G]. Additional memory is required for the lookup table in [Reflected Light Component Lookup](3521-reflected-light-component-lookup.md) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** The method is described as "nearly interactive" when combined with the lookup table technique [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles noise and nonlinearity better than binary thresholding [S1, S2, unofficial]. Interprets gradient in context of data value, avoiding false positives for high-gradient interior voxels [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate gradient estimation [G]. Performance depends on the complexity of the shading lookup [G].
-*   **When to Use:** When realistic surface shading is required for volume data with distinct material boundaries [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When only ambient shading is sufficient, or when gradient information is unreliable [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel for the classification step. The calculation involves basic arithmetic operations (absolute values, multiplications, additions, comparisons).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel for the classification step. No additional storage is required beyond the input data and gradient.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Accurately identifies surface boundaries by considering the data value range, avoiding false positives from high gradients in the volume interior. Handles noise and nonlinearity via probability ramps.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate gradient computation. Relies on linear approximation of the field function, which may be inaccurate for highly nonlinear fields. The scaling factor $k$ requires tuning.
+*   **When to Use:** When accurate surface shading is required for specific materials in volume rendering, especially when gradient magnitude alone leads to noisy or incorrect surface detection.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and simple thresholding is sufficient, or when the field function is highly nonlinear and the linear approximation is invalid.
 
 ## Background
-The method is part of the Voltx system [S2, unofficial]. It departs from earlier work [Lev90a, LL94] by interpreting gradient magnitude in the context of data value and classification parameters [S1, S2, unofficial]. It was developed to eliminate technical programming used by previously reported 3D texture-map methods [S2, unofficial].
+The method was introduced in the context of Direct Volume Rendering with Shading via Three-Dimensional Textures [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It departs from earlier work [Lev90a, LL94] that used gradient magnitude in isolation for surface detection [S1, S3, unofficial]. The method is part of a pipeline that includes Reflected Light Component Lookup and Gradient Quantization to achieve interactive rendering with directional lighting [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
